  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After hearing Vika's complaint, Nagiyev teases Chef Barinov about getting old and needing to retire. Meanwhile, in the locker room, Max notices an old army scar on Lyova before a mysterious foreign visitor arrives asking for Katya.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ты уже стареешь, дорогой Витя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Раньше ты так долго не думал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Может, тебе уже пора отдыхать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Это очень смешно, Дмитрий Владимирович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты слишком часто стал шутить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Любишь ты шутить каждый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Тебя в зале спрашивает гость.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Я сказал, что ты здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Пойдёмте, Виктория Сергеевна, выпьем кофе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Оставьте нашего старого шефа одного.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего себе, какой большой шрам!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Да, это было давно в армии.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что тебе надо? Ничего, работаем.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ты уже стареешь, дорогой Витя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you getting old already, dear Vitya?' Notice how Russian expresses 'you' and 'Vitya' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Раньше ты так долго не думал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Before, you did not take this long to think.' The meaning hinges on 'earlier' working together with 'thought' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Может, тебе уже пора отдыхать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe it is already time for you to rest?' Notice how Russian expresses 'maybe' and 'to rest' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Это очень смешно, Дмитрий Владимирович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is very funny, Dmitry Vladimirovich.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'this', while 'ridiculous' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты слишком часто стал шутить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You have started joking too often.' Notice how Russian expresses 'you' and 'to joke' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Любишь ты шутить каждый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You love to joke every day.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'love' and 'you' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Тебя в зале спрашивает гость.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A guest is asking for you in the dining room.' In Russian, the idea of 'you' flows naturally into 'guest' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Я сказал, что ты здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I said that you are here.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'here'. Dmitry Nagiyev answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Пойдёмте, Виктория Сергеевна, выпьем кофе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us go, Viktoria Sergeyevna, and drink coffee.' Notice how Russian expresses 'let's go' and 'coffee' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Оставьте нашего старого шефа одного.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Leave our old chef alone.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'leave' before landing on 'alone'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего себе, какой большой шрам!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wow, what a big scar!' Max brings out the contrast between 'nothing' and 'to oneself' in a few short words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Да, это было давно в армии.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, that was a long time ago in the army.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'yes', while 'army' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что тебе надо? Ничего, работаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you want? Nothing, we are working.' The meaning hinges on 'what' working together with 'let's work' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
